---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as a warehouse receiving and inventory-control specialist. Help me plan an evidence-based investigation; do not pretend to have inspected goods or checked records.

TASK:
Compare the expected delivery, physical receipt and system posting. Identify what needs verification before receipt correction, stock release or escalation.

RECEIPT CONTEXT:
[Purchase order, transfer, return or other receipt; warehouse and receiving location. Use anonymized references.]

EXPECTED DELIVERY:
[SKU, quantity due on THIS delivery, UOM, confirmed pack conversion, PO/ASN/delivery-note quantities. Distinguish total order quantity from scheduled or remaining delivery quantity.]

PHYSICAL COUNT:
[Count by SKU, location and condition; count time; counted/recounted by role; batch, expiry or serial details where relevant. State whether damaged or held units are included in the total.]

SYSTEM RECEIPT:
[Quantity, UOM, location, status and posting time; unposted, partial, completed, reversed or unknown. Include earlier partial receipts where relevant.]

DISCREPANCY:
[Shortage, overage, wrong SKU, damage, UOM, batch/expiry, serial, label or system posting issue.]

EVIDENCE:
[Count sheets, documents, labels, photos, seal records, scan logs or transaction history. Describe what is available and what remains unverified.]

ACTIONS ALREADY TAKEN:
[Recount, segregation, hold, put-away, receipt posting or escalation.]

APPLICABLE CONTROLS:
[Provided acceptance rules, tolerances, approval roles, quality requirements and claim deadlines. Write unknown where not supplied.]

COST AND IMPACT, IF KNOWN:
[Unit cost and currency, affected orders or production. Do not supply confidential data unnecessarily.]

INVESTIGATION RULES:
- Separate supplied facts, calculations, hypotheses and checks still needed.
- Use the same SKU, UOM, delivery scope and cut-off time for each comparison. Ask for missing conversions; never invent pack sizes.
- A PO states an order quantity, not proof of what was loaded or delivered. A matching seal alone does not establish the loaded quantity or responsibility.
- Missing, damaged, held and available quantities are different. Do not double-count damaged units as both missing and physically received.
- Do not assume a root cause, invent evidence, simulate completed checks or assign blame.
- No AI output authorizes receipt posting, adjustment, release, rejection, return or a claim. Identify the decision and the role that must confirm it under supplied procedures.

1. RECONCILE THE QUANTITIES
Show a table for each affected SKU with expected delivery, physical total, damaged/held subset and system receipt. Mark unknown values explicitly.
Calculate signed delivery variance = physical total minus expected delivery.
A negative value is a shortage; a positive value is an overage.
Calculate system-versus-physical difference only when both quantities cover the same receipt and status scope. An unposted receipt is a pending transaction, not automatically an inventory shortage.
Show working and units. Calculate value only with a provided cost in the same UOM and a stated currency. Separate missing-stock value from damaged-stock value; neither establishes a recoverable claim.

2. PRIORITIZE VERIFICATION
Start with an independent recount and confirmation of SKU, UOM, pack size and delivery scope. Check staging, adjacent locations, mixed pallets, loose units and earlier partial receipts.
Compare PO, ASN, delivery note, packing list and physical labels; do not assume any document proves the physical count.
Review system receipt identifiers, quantities, UOM, timestamps, locations, reversals and duplicate or pending postings.
For each check, state the question, evidence required, proposed role and what the result would change. Mark roles as proposed if responsibility was not provided.

3. CHECK THE RELEVANT EXCEPTIONS
Use only applicable branches; do not fill every branch with generic findings.
- Shortage/overage: examine loading and unloading counts, partial deliveries, mixed deliveries and counting errors.
- Wrong SKU/UOM/label: compare actual product identifiers, supplier codes, barcode mappings and verified conversion factors.
- Damage: record affected quantity and condition, preserve packaging/photos and review handling evidence. Do not infer when damage occurred from appearance alone.
- Batch/expiry/serial: compare documents, labels, physical identifiers and system records; apply only supplied acceptance rules.
- Seal/transport: compare seal and vehicle records and available handover evidence without assuming liability.

4. ASSESS EVIDENCE AND CAUSES
List each plausible explanation with supporting evidence, contradictory or missing evidence and the next check.
Keep the cause unresolved where evidence is insufficient. Use 5 Whys only when each link has support; label unanswered questions rather than inventing a chain.
Distinguish the quantity discrepancy from any demonstrated process failure.

5. PLAN CONTAINMENT AND DECISIONS
Identify any need to preserve goods, packaging and records, segregate affected stock or seek a quality/supervisor decision under the provided controls.
Distinguish an initial receipt from a reversal, receipt correction or stock adjustment. Do not propose an additional adjustment that would duplicate a transaction correction.
List what must be verified and approved before posting, release, rejection, return or adjustment. If rules are unknown, ask for confirmation rather than inventing policy or stopping unrelated stock automatically.
For escalation or a potential claim, list missing documents, applicable deadline to confirm and responsible role. Do not assert liability or claim validity.

6. PREVENT RECURRENCE
Tie corrective actions to supported findings. For each, give a proposed owner role, completion evidence and effectiveness check. Leave due dates and approval thresholds to the organization when not supplied.
If cause is unresolved, prioritize evidence collection over speculative corrective actions.

OUTPUT:
1. Brief discrepancy summary and urgent questions
2. Quantity reconciliation with calculations
3. Prioritized investigation checklist: check | evidence | proposed role | decision affected
4. Facts, hypotheses and evidence gaps
5. Containment, posting and approval decisions still required
6. Corrective actions and follow-up checks
7. Unresolved questions and investigation status

Keep the response concise and specific to this receipt. Before finishing, check the arithmetic, avoid double-counting and confirm no hypothesis has been presented as a verified finding.`;

const steps = [
  {
    "title": "Define this delivery",
    "description": "Enter the quantity due on this delivery, not just the total PO quantity. Include the SKU, unit, pack size, count time and any earlier partial receipts."
  },
  {
    "title": "Separate the three records",
    "description": "Provide expected, physically counted and system-posted quantities separately. Say whether damaged or held stock is included in the physical total."
  },
  {
    "title": "Ask for checks, then gather evidence",
    "description": "Use anonymized notes. Have AI list evidence gaps and proposed checks; receiving staff must perform the recount and verify the documents and transactions."
  },
  {
    "title": "Review before posting or release",
    "description": "Check the arithmetic and proposed actions against the actual receipt and approval procedure. Keep unsupported causes unresolved and record who approved the next action."
  }
];

const example = {
  "input": [
    "Fictional training example: SKU REC-01, one delivery due for 120 cartons. PO delivery schedule and delivery note both show 120 cartons; no previous partial receipt.",
    "Physical count: 118 cartons at the receiving dock, independently recounted. This total includes 3 visibly damaged cartons; 115 have no visible external damage. All counts use the same carton UOM.",
    "The seal number matches the paperwork. Supplier loading counts and internal carton contents have not been verified.",
    "No system receipt has been posted. The 3 damaged cartons are segregated pending review. Acceptance rules, unit cost and claim deadlines have not been supplied.",
    "Prepare an investigation plan. Do not decide supplier responsibility or authorize posting or release."
  ],
  "output": [
    "Delivery variance: 118 \u2212 120 = \u22122 cartons, a shortage of 2 cartons against the stated delivery quantity. The 3 damaged cartons are included in the 118 received; they are not another 3 missing cartons.",
    "Condition split: 118 \u2212 3 = 115 cartons without visible external damage and 3 visibly damaged cartons. This does not establish that 115 cartons are approved for release or that their contents are correct.",
    "System status: Receipt is unposted. Do not classify all 118 cartons as a system discrepancy or propose a stock adjustment simply because posting is pending.",
    "Next checks: Receiving staff should verify carton identifiers and contents as appropriate, check staging and mixed-delivery locations, and compare pallet counts with loading and handover records. Record results against this delivery reference.",
    "Cause remains unresolved: Matching seal details do not prove that 120 cartons were loaded. Supplier short loading, transport events and receiving-location errors remain hypotheses requiring evidence.",
    "Decision required: Confirm the approved treatment of the 2-carton shortage and 3 damaged cartons with the responsible receiving/quality roles. Record any authorized receipt and hold status accurately; do not post 120 to match the document.",
    "Value and claims: No unit cost or claim terms were supplied. Do not calculate a loss value or declare a valid claim. Confirm required evidence and deadlines with the appropriate procurement role."
  ]
};

const tips = [
  {
    "title": "Compare the same delivery",
    "description": "An order may arrive in several deliveries. Match references and cut-off times before treating the outstanding order balance as missing stock."
  },
  {
    "title": "Separate quantity from condition",
    "description": "Damaged cartons can still be physically present. Record their condition and status without subtracting them twice or assuming other goods are approved for release."
  },
  {
    "title": "Treat documents as evidence to examine",
    "description": "A delivery note, seal number or signed acknowledgement has limits. Ask what each record supports and what it cannot establish."
  },
  {
    "title": "Record the approved outcome",
    "description": "Keep recount results, evidence references, the authorized receipt or correction, approver and follow-up checks together so another reviewer can trace the decision."
  }
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Receiving Discrepancy Investigation Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Investigate warehouse receiving shortages, overages, wrong SKUs, UOM errors, damage, batch, expiry, labeling and system receipt discrepancies with a structured corrective-action process."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="OPERATIONS & LOGISTICS"
    title="Receiving Discrepancy"
    accentTitle="Investigation."
    description="Investigate receiving shortages, overages, wrong items, UOM errors, damage, batch and expiry problems using documentary, physical and system evidence."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Warehouse receiving",
      "Discrepancy investigation",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Receiving teams",
      "Inventory controllers",
      "Warehouse supervisors",
      "3PL operations",
      "Supplier discrepancies",
      "Inbound investigations",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Confirm the discrepancy before assigning the cause."
    introDescription="Replace the bracketed fields with anonymized receipt details. Ask AI to reconcile quantities and plan the checks; your team must verify the evidence and approve any receiving decision."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="120 expected, 118 received, 3 damaged."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Protect receiving accuracy and evidence."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Build a receiving prompt."
    sidebarDescription="Use the Prompt Builder to organize your receiving context, evidence, constraints and required output before working with your AI assistant."
    warningTitle="Preserve evidence before adjustment"
    warningText="Receiving discrepancies can become difficult to verify once goods are moved, mixed, put away or inventory transactions are corrected. Preserve relevant physical, documentary and system evidence before making adjustments."
  />
</BaseLayout>